Here's a simple Java program that implements the palindrome check functionality:

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        
        // Convert string to array of characters for easier manipulation
        char[] arr = s.toCharArray();
        
        // Initialize two pointers, one at the start and one at the end of the array
        int left = 0;
        int right = n - 1;
        
        // Loop through the array until the two pointers meet
        while (left < right) {
            if (arr[left] != arr[right]) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        
        return true;
    }

    public boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        
        // Convert string to array of characters for easier manipulation
        char[] arr = s.toCharArray();
        
        // Initialize two pointers, one at the start and one at the end of the array
        int left = 0;
        int right = n - 1;
        
        // Loop through the array until the two pointers meet
        while (left < right) {
            if (!Character.equals(arr[left], arr[right])) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        
        return true;
    }

    public boolean isPalindrome(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        
        // Convert array of integers to array of characters for easier manipulation
        char[] arr = new char[n];
        System.arraycopy(nums, 0, arr, 0, n);
        
        // Initialize two pointers, one at the start and one at the end of the array
        int left = 0;
        int right = n - 1;
        
        // Loop through the array until the two pointers meet
        while (left < right) {
            if (!Character.equals(arr[left], arr[right])) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        
        return true;
    }

    public boolean isPalindrome(int[] nums, int start, int end) {
        // If the start index is greater than or equal to the end index,
        // it means there are no characters in the array to compare
        if (start >= end) {
            return true;
        }
        
        while (start <= end) {
            if (!Character.equals(nums[start], nums[end])) {
                return false;
            }
            start++;
            end--;
        }
        
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution solution = new Solution();

        System.out.println(solution.isPalindrome("radar"));  // true
        System.out.println(solution.isPalindrome("hello"));  // false
        System.out.println(solution.isPalindrome(12321));  // true
        System.out.println(solution.isPalindrome(123456));  // false

        solution = new Solution();
        System.out.println(solution.isPalindrome(12125));  // true
    }
}
```

In this program, we first convert the string or array of integers to an array of characters for easier manipulation. We then use two pointers, one at the start and one at the end of the array, and compare characters from both ends until they meet in the middle. If any pair of characters do not match, we return false; otherwise, we return true.

For example, when checking if "radar" is a palindrome, we start by comparing characters from the beginning and end of the string, moving towards the center. Since "r-d-a-r" is a palindrome, our function returns true.
